@page "/getting-started"

<PageTitle>Getting started · Bmotion</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Getting started</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        One package and one service registration - then any element on an interactively rendered page can
        spring, fade, follow the pointer or animate out before it is removed. All of the maths runs in C#;
        the small browser bridge loads itself the first time an animation runs.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Install</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The package targets .NET 10 and 11. Its browser bridge, <code>bit-bmotion.js</code>, ships as a static
        web asset and is imported on demand, so there is no <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag to add and no
        JavaScript to write. In a Blazor Web App the package belongs in the project whose components animate -
        the <code>.Client</code> project for WebAssembly pages.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">dotnet add package Bit.Bmotion</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Register the services</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A Blazor Web App has two DI containers: the client's components are instantiated once on the server for
        the prerender pass and again in the browser, so the registration goes in both <code>Program.cs</code> files.
        This site keeps it in one extension method that both call. Outside AOT builds, a WebAssembly app can also
        <a href="getting-started#lazy-loading">lazy load</a> the library instead of registering it.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">// Program.cs - in every project that renders animations</span>
<span class="k">using</span> Bit.Bmotion;

builder.Services.AddBitBmotionServices();</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The options are global. The two worth deciding on day one are how <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> is
        honoured - <code>BmReducedMotionMode.User</code> is the recommended setting - and what prerendered markup
        shows before the runtime takes over:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">builder.Services.AddBitBmotionServices(o =&gt;
{
    o.ReducedMotion = BmReducedMotionMode.User;   <span class="c">// follow the OS everywhere</span>
    o.Prerender = BmPrerenderMode.Animate;        <span class="c">// play enter animations from the served HTML</span>
});</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The other two are safety switches with sensible defaults. <code>CssSafeMode</code> checks the string
        values a target carries - colours, sizes, shadows, filters - before they are written into the inline
        style, and is worth turning on wherever any of them come from user input. <code>InertWhileExiting</code>,
        on by default, makes content that is playing its exit animation <code>inert</code>, so a closing dialog
        cannot be clicked, focused or read out a second time.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">builder.Services.AddBitBmotionServices(o =&gt;
{
    o.CssSafeMode = BmCssSafeMode.Throw;   <span class="c">// Off (default), Warn or Throw on an unsafe CSS string</span>
    o.InertWhileExiting = true;            <span class="c">// the default: exiting content can't be interacted with</span>
});</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Make the components reachable</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        One line in <code>_Imports.razor</code>, in every project that renders animations. Without it
        <code>&lt;Bmotion&gt;</code> is not a known component and the build fails with RZ10012.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="k">@@using</span> Bit.Bmotion</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Pick a render mode</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Animations need an interactive render mode. <strong>InteractiveWebAssembly</strong> supports every feature,
        because the engine can run its per-frame loop in the browser - it is what this site uses.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
        On <strong>Blazor Server</strong> the browser plays the same animations: transforms, opacity, colours,
        sizes, filters, keyframes, springs, inertia and arcs, on enter, exit, gestures and variants alike. Drag
        stays under the pointer, with <code>OnDrag</code> a round trip behind. The two things that need the
        per-frame loop - per-frame callbacks and SVG path morphs - become an instant change. Inject
        <code>BmotionCapabilities</code> to tell which runtime you are on rather than guessing; the
        <a href="accessibility">Accessibility</a> page shows it live.
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Prerendered enter animations</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        A prerendered page has no runtime yet, so by default an element is served at its <code>Initial</code> state
        and animates once the page is interactive - which for <code>Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0)"</code> means
        invisible content until the runtime has downloaded. With <code>BmPrerenderMode.Animate</code> the enter
        animation is compiled to CSS and plays straight from the served HTML; it needs the library's stylesheet in
        the page head. <code>BmPrerenderMode.Final</code> serves elements at rest instead, with no stylesheet.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">&lt;!-- App.razor, in &lt;head&gt; - only needed for BmPrerenderMode.Animate --&gt;</span>
&lt;<span class="k">link</span> rel=<span class="s">"stylesheet"</span> href=<span class="s">"_content/Bit.Bmotion/bit-bmotion.css"</span> /&gt;</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The option is the default for every element, and any one element can choose differently with its own
        <code>Prerender</code> parameter - for instance the headline that must be readable, and indexable, the
        moment the HTML arrives, on a page whose other content animates in. Whichever mode prerendered it, an
        element that was served at rest is taken over at rest, so its entrance doesn't play a second time.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">Bmotion</span> Prerender=<span class="s">"BmPrerenderMode.Final"</span>
         Initial=<span class="s">"Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 20)"</span> Animate=<span class="s">"Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"</span>&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">h1</span>&gt;Visible in the served HTML&lt;/<span class="k">h1</span>&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">Bmotion</span>&gt;</pre>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> A first animation</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        <code>&lt;Bmotion&gt;</code> renders no element of its own: it animates the one element inside it.
        <code>Initial</code> is where that element starts, <code>Animate</code> is where it is going, and
        <code>Transition</code> says how it gets there. Gesture states such as <code>WhileHover</code> and
        <code>WhileTap</code> layer on top and revert by themselves.
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code">&lt;<span class="k">Bmotion</span> Initial=<span class="s">"Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 20)"</span>
         Animate=<span class="s">"Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"</span>
         WhileHover=<span class="s">"Bm.To(scale: 1.1)"</span>
         WhileTap=<span class="s">"Bm.To(scale: 0.95)"</span>
         Transition=<span class="s">"Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.4, duration: 0.6)"</span>&gt;
    &lt;<span class="k">div</span> class=<span class="s">"box"</span>&gt;Hello&lt;/<span class="k">div</span>&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">Bmotion</span>&gt;</pre>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Live &middot; hover it, press it</span>
            <Bmotion @key="_run"
                     Initial="Bm.To(opacity: 0, y: 20)"
                     Animate="Bm.To(opacity: 1, y: 0)"
                     WhileHover="Bm.To(scale: 1.1)"
                     WhileTap="Bm.To(scale: 0.95)"
                     Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.4, duration: 0.6)">
                <div class="box box-label">Hello</div>
            </Bmotion>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="() => _run++">Replay the entrance</button>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card" id="lazy-loading">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Lazy loading on WebAssembly</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The components also work without <code>AddBitBmotionServices</code>, so a WebAssembly app can fetch Bmotion
        only when someone opens a part of the app that animates. When the services are not registered,
        <code>Bmotion</code>, <code>BmotionReorderGroup</code> and <code>BmotionInspector</code> create every service
        they need themselves, once per WebAssembly runtime; <code>IServiceProvider.GetBmotionService&lt;T&gt;()</code>
        hands the app's own code the same ones, and <code>IServiceProvider.ConfigureBmotionServices(...)</code> sets
        their options. Mark the assembly as lazy loaded in the project that runs in the browser, and load it before the
        first page that uses it renders:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">&lt;!-- The browser project's .csproj --&gt;</span>
&lt;<span class="k">ItemGroup</span>&gt;
  &lt;<span class="k">BlazorWebAssemblyLazyLoad</span> Include=<span class="s">"Bit.Bmotion.wasm"</span> /&gt;
&lt;/<span class="k">ItemGroup</span>&gt;</pre>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">@@* App.razor - in a Blazor Web App, the client project's Routes.razor: the router waits for
   OnNavigateAsync before it renders the page. *@@</span>
<span class="k">@@using</span> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Services
<span class="k">@@inject</span> LazyAssemblyLoader LazyAssemblyLoader

&lt;<span class="k">Router</span> AppAssembly=<span class="s">"typeof(Program).Assembly"</span> OnNavigateAsync=<span class="s">"OnNavigateAsync"</span>&gt;
    ...
&lt;/<span class="k">Router</span>&gt;

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="k">private async</span> Task OnNavigateAsync(NavigationContext context)
    {
        <span class="c">// The pages that animate: fetch Bmotion before they render. A second call loads nothing again.</span>
        <span class="k">if</span> (context.Path.StartsWith(<span class="s">"showcase"</span>, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            <span class="k">await</span> LazyAssemblyLoader.LoadAssembliesAsync([<span class="s">"Bit.Bmotion.wasm"</span>]);
    }
}</pre>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Those pages, or a layout only they use, hold the animated elements as usual. The layout sets the options
        <code>AddBitBmotionServices</code> would have - where the services are registered, as on the server of a Blazor
        Web App prerendering it, the call does nothing - and a component that animates from code takes its service from
        <code>GetBmotionService&lt;T&gt;()</code> instead of injecting it:
    </p>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">@@* ShowcaseLayout.razor *@@</span>
<span class="k">@@inherits</span> LayoutComponentBase
<span class="k">@@inject</span> IServiceProvider Services

<span class="k">@@Body</span>

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="c">// Runs before the elements of the page mount.</span>
    <span class="k">protected override void</span> OnInitialized() =&gt; Services.ConfigureBmotionServices(o =&gt;
    {
        o.ReducedMotion = BmReducedMotionMode.User;
        o.Prerender = BmPrerenderMode.Animate;
    });
}</pre>
    <pre class="bb-code"><span class="c">@@* Counter.razor *@@</span>
<span class="k">@@inject</span> IServiceProvider Services

<span class="k">@@code</span> {
    <span class="c">// Taken once, as an injected service would be.</span>
    <span class="k">private</span> BmotionAnimateService Motion =&gt; <span class="k">field</span> ??= Services.GetBmotionService&lt;BmotionAnimateService&gt;();
}</pre>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Leave <code>AddBitBmotionServices</code> out of that project's <code>Program.cs</code>: calling it references Bmotion's types before the assembly is there. For the same reason only what renders after the assembly has loaded may use Bmotion's types - the pages it is loaded for and a layout only they use, not <code>App.razor</code>, <code>Routes.razor</code>, a layout every page shares or <code>Program.cs</code>.</li>
        <li>No type in an assembly the router reads when the app starts - the app's own assembly first of all - may derive from or implement one of Bmotion's: no component that inherits <code>BmotionAnimatePresence</code> or <code>BmotionConfig</code>, no <code>IBmotionInterop</code> of your own. On WebAssembly such a type fails to load before Bit.Bmotion is there, and keeps failing after it has loaded. Put it in a library that is lazy loaded together with Bit.Bmotion.</li>
        <li>Until <code>ConfigureBmotionServices</code> sets them, the elements run on the default <code>BitBmotionOptions</code>. They are shared by every element of the runtime and every call applies its configuration again, so configure them in one place, the same way every time. Per element, <code>Prerender</code> and a surrounding <code>&lt;BmotionConfig ReduceMotion&gt;</code> still override them.</li>
        <li>In a Blazor Web App only the browser lazy loads. Give <code>Routes</code> the <code>InteractiveWebAssembly</code> or <code>InteractiveAuto</code> render mode in <code>App.razor</code>, so the router - and its <code>OnNavigateAsync</code> - runs in the browser. The server project keeps its <code>AddBitBmotionServices()</code> and registers <code>LazyAssemblyLoader</code>, which <code>Routes.razor</code> injects while it is prerendered there: <code>builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;LazyAssemblyLoader&gt;()</code>. Configure the same <code>Prerender</code> in the browser as on the server, or elements prerendered at rest play their entrance a second time.</li>
        <li>Do not lazy load in an AOT build. AOT compiles every assembly into <code>dotnet.native.wasm</code>, so there is nothing left to download later, and lazy loading an AOT-compiled assembly can fail at startup.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

